const studyGuide={
people:{title:"Australia & its people",summary:"Build a chronological and symbolic map of Australia, then connect dates and places to meaning.",lineage:[["Deep roots","Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander peoples are Australia’s First Peoples. Their continuing cultures and connections to Country pre-date British settlement by many thousands of years."],["British settlement","The First Fleet arrived in 1788. This is a settlement milestone, not the date Australia became a federation."],["Colonies to Commonwealth","Six self-governing colonies agreed to federate. On 1 January 1901 the Commonwealth of Australia was formed."],["National identity today","States, territories, capitals, flags, commemorative days and symbols provide the civic geography and shared reference points used throughout the test."]],insight:"The exam often rewards distinguishing nearby ideas: settlement is not Federation; Canberra is the national capital even though Sydney and Melbourne are larger cities.",cue:"When you see a date, ask: settlement, gold-era change, Federation, or commemoration?"},
rights:{title:"Democratic beliefs, rights & liberties",summary:"Understand the balance: people have freedoms, but those freedoms operate within a common legal framework.",lineage:[["Representative democracy","Australia’s parliamentary democracy gives citizens a say by electing representatives and participating in public life."],["Rule of law","Government authority and individual freedom both sit under law. No person or group is above Australian law."],["Freedoms","Speech, religion and association allow people to hold and express different lawful views and affiliations."],["Equality and participation","Equality of opportunity, mutual respect and the idea of a fair go support participation across a diverse society."]],insight:"Freedom and law are not opposites in the test material. The recurring pattern is freedom within law, plus respect for the equal rights of others.",cue:"For scenario questions, reject options that use force, discrimination or claim a personal rule overrides Australian law."},
government:{title:"Government & the law",summary:"Separate institutions by function. Most confusion disappears once you ask who makes, executes or interprets a rule.",lineage:[["Constitutional framework","The Australian Constitution establishes the Commonwealth system and provides the framework for national government."],["Legislative power","Parliament makes laws. A proposed new law is a Bill."],["Executive power","The executive puts laws into action through government and administration."],["Judicial power","Courts interpret and apply laws independently when deciding cases."],["Levels of government","Federal, state or territory, and local government divide public responsibilities by scale rather than by the three powers."]],insight:"The three powers and three levels are two different maps. Powers describe function. Levels describe which sphere of government handles an area.",cue:"MAKE → DO → JUDGE maps to Legislative → Executive → Judicial."},
values:{title:"Australian Values",summary:"Treat Values as a decision system, not a vocabulary list. Apply the principles to behaviour.",lineage:[["Freedom and dignity","People are entitled to lawful freedom and should respect the dignity of others."],["Equality","Men and women have equal rights, and equality of opportunity is a core expectation."],["Peaceful democracy","Change should be pursued through voting, discussion, lawful campaigning and peaceful protest rather than violence."],["Law and shared obligations","Australian law applies to everyone. Cultural, family or religious rules do not displace it."],["Community","Mutual respect, participation and responsibility help people with different backgrounds live together peacefully."]],insight:"Values questions are deliberately practical. The safest reasoning pattern is not to memorise slogans but to test whether an action is lawful, peaceful, respectful and equal.",cue:"VALUES FILTER → Lawful? Peaceful? Respectful? Equal?"}
};
